(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-cleanenergyspeed"]={"plain":{"bills":{"cleanenergyspeed":{"slug":"cleanenergyspeed","title":"The Clean Energy Speed Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-1","heading":"\u00a7 1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the Clean Energy Speed Act."},{"anchor":"sec-2","heading":"\u00a7 2. Findings","part":null,"body":"**(1)** Utility-scale solar and onshore wind are the least expensive new sources of electric generation available in the United States on an unsubsidized basis, and the cost of new gas-fired generation reached a ten-year high in 2025.\n\n**(2)** A residential solar energy system that costs less than one dollar per watt to install in Australia, before any subsidy, costs a median of approximately $3.60 per watt to install in the United States, before any incentive. Non-hardware cost, including customer acquisition, overhead, and the permitting, inspection, and interconnection process, is approximately three times as high per watt in the United States as in Australia and accounts for approximately half of the difference; higher hardware prices account for the rest.\n\n**(3)** Where United States jurisdictions have adopted automated permitting for standardized residential solar systems, permits that took weeks issue in hours, at lower cost to the jurisdiction and to the homeowner, with no measured change in safety outcomes. More than 160 jurisdictions have done so voluntarily.\n\n**(4)** More than one million small solar devices that plug into an ordinary outlet operate lawfully in Germany, under a national technical standard and without individual utility approval. In 2025 Utah enacted the first State law authorizing such devices, by a unanimous vote of both houses.\n\n**(5)** Congress has three times established that a certified article may circulate nationally without being re-examined by every local jurisdiction it enters: manufactured housing built to the Federal construction standard under section 5403 of title 42, United States Code; consumer appliances under section 6297 of title 42, United States Code; and antenna devices under section 207 of the Telecommunications Act of 1996 and the regulations under it. Each regime has operated for decades.\n\n**(6)** Most solar and wind generation is built on private land and requires no Federal environmental review. Federal review governs three classes of energy project \u2014 projects on Federal land, projects on the outer Continental Shelf, and Federal authorizations for transmission \u2014 and it is in those classes that review time is a measured obstacle.\n\n**(7)** Section 107 of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969, as added in 2023, sets deadlines of one year for an environmental assessment and two years for an environmental impact statement, but permits the lead agency to extend its own deadline, does not bound the number or length of extensions, and supplies no consequence for a missed deadline other than a suit the applicant must bring.\n\n**(8)** In 2025, stop-work orders were issued against every offshore wind project then under construction in Federal waters, each project having every Federal authorization the law requires. By February 2026 a court had enjoined every such order. The authority invoked for the orders was the unbounded discretion over suspension and cancellation that section 8(p) of the Outer Continental Shelf Lands Act leaves to regulation.\n\n**(9)** A certified small energy system is an article of commerce manufactured, listed, distributed, and sold in interstate commerce, and the variance of thousands of local permitting processes with respect to an identical certified article is a burden on that commerce which Congress has power to remove.\n\n**(10)** In 2024 a State regulatory authority authorized a utility to impose on residential customers with rooftop solar systems a charge that other residential customers do not pay, while determining that the utility provides those customers no unique service. California and Nevada prohibit any charge that raises a customer-generator's costs above those of customers in the rate class to which it would belong without its system. A charge imposed by reason of a certified small energy system raises the cost of operating an article described in paragraph (9)."},{"anchor":"sec-3","heading":"\u00a7 3. Definitions","part":null,"body":"**(a) Covered clean energy project.** A **covered clean energy project** is a facility, or an addition or alteration to a facility, for \u2014 **(1)** the generation of electric energy without the combustion or other chemical conversion of a carbon-bearing fuel; **(2)** the storage of electric energy, however accomplished; or **(3)** the transmission or distribution of electric energy, including the replacement of conductors and the installation of equipment that increases the capacity or controllability of an existing line. **No other facility is a covered clean energy project, and the omission of any facility that generates electric energy by the combustion of a fuel, with or without the capture of carbon, is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(b) Qualifying site.** A **qualifying site** is \u2014 **(1)** land previously used for resource extraction, industrial operations, or electric generation, including a mine site, a well pad, a brownfield site, and the site of a generating station retired or scheduled for retirement; **(2)** the developed footprint and legal right-of-way of an existing transmission or distribution line, road, rail line, pipeline, or canal; **(3)** land within the developed footprint of an existing covered clean energy project, for repowering or expansion; and **(4)** a rooftop, parking structure, or other constructed surface. **Land is not a qualifying site by designation, and no agency may add to or subtract from this subsection by rule.**\n\n**(c) Certified small energy system.** A **certified small energy system** is a solar photovoltaic system, an energy storage system, or a combination of the two, that \u2014 **(1)** has a nameplate capacity not exceeding the capacity set in the schedule to this Act; **(2)** is composed of equipment listed by a nationally recognized testing laboratory to the standard governing that equipment; and **(3)** is installed in conformity with the National Electrical Code as adopted in the jurisdiction, or, where the jurisdiction has adopted no edition of that code within the period set in the schedule to this Act, the most recent edition.\n\n**(d) Plug-in energy system.** A **plug-in energy system** is a solar photovoltaic device, with or without integrated storage, that is listed by a nationally recognized testing laboratory to a standard governing plug-connected generating devices, is connected through an ordinary attachment plug, and does not exceed **the listed power limit of the standard to which it is listed, as that standard is revised.** No power limit is stated in this Act, and the limit of this subsection rises or falls with the standard.\n\n**(e) Permitting authority.** A **permitting authority** is a State, a political subdivision of a State, or any agency or instrumentality of either, that requires a permit, review, inspection, approval, or fee as a condition of installing or operating an energy system. **The term does not include an Indian Tribe, and that omission is a determination of Congress.**\n\n**(f) Covered Federal authorization.** A **covered Federal authorization** is a permit, lease, right-of-way, easement, certification, opinion, or other approval issued by a Federal agency with respect to a covered clean energy project.\n\n**(g) Record complete.** The record on an application is **complete** on the earliest date by which the agency or permitting authority has received the application and every study, document, and fee that a statute or a rule published before the application was filed makes a condition of decision. **A request for material not so published does not delay completeness**, and completeness is not defeated by the pendency of the agency's own analysis.\n\n**(h) Utility.** A **utility** is a person engaged in the distribution or sale of electric energy to the public, including a State or municipal instrumentality and a cooperative.\n\n**(i) Review deadline.** The **review deadline** of an environmental document is the deadline that section 107(g) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336a(g)) sets for it, as extended under that subsection as amended by \u00a7 5, or, where a fee has been paid with respect to the document under section 112 of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336f), the period that section establishes. **A schedule or deadline set by any court, or by any consent decree, settlement, or agreement, including one under \u00a7 5(b)(2) or section 107(g)(3) of that Act, but not an extension under section 107(g)(2) of that Act as amended by \u00a7 5(b)(1), is a remedy for a missed review deadline and extends no review deadline.**"},{"anchor":"sec-4","heading":"\u00a7 4. Site-based categorical exclusions","part":null,"body":"**(a) The rule.** A covered Federal authorization for a covered clean energy project located wholly on one or more qualifying sites **is categorically excluded from the requirement to prepare an environmental assessment or an environmental impact statement** under the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969. The exclusion of this subsection **applies by operation of this section**, and no agency adoption, rulemaking, or listing is a condition of it.\n\n**(b) Extraordinary circumstances.** An agency may prepare an environmental assessment for an action described in subsection (a) only on a written determination, published with the decision, identifying the specific circumstance of the particular site that takes the action outside the ordinary case. **A determination that recites the possibility of effects common to the class \u2014 visual change, construction noise, vehicle traffic, or the ordinary presence of wildlife \u2014 does not satisfy this subsection.**\n\n**(c) One-way boundary.** No agency may by rule, guidance, or practice extend the exclusion of subsection (a) to land that is not a qualifying site. **This subsection is a limit on every agency and may not be waived.**\n\n**(d) Other exclusions preserved.** This section does not narrow any categorical exclusion established by statute or by an agency before or after enactment."},{"anchor":"sec-5","heading":"\u00a7 5. Review deadlines \u2014 completion of the clock Congress already set","part":null,"body":"**(a) Start of review periods.** With respect to an application for a covered Federal authorization, each review period under section 107(g)(1) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336a(g)(1)) runs from the earlier of the date on which it would begin under that paragraph and the date on which the record on the application is complete under \u00a7 3(g). Where a fee has been paid with respect to an environmental document under section 112 of that Act (42 U.S.C. 4336f), the period under subsection (a)(4) of that section runs from the earlier of the date on which it would begin under that subsection and the date on which the record on the application is complete under \u00a7 3(g). **These rules apply notwithstanding those provisions and are subject to \u00a7 19(b).**\n\n**(b) Amendment.** Section 107(g) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336a(g)) is amended so that, with respect to a covered clean energy project \u2014\n\n> **(1)** a lead agency may extend a deadline under that subsection **once**, by not more than 30 days, and only on a written determination, published on the date it is made, stating the specific analytic task that remains, why it could not be completed within the deadline, and the date certain on which it will be completed. **A determination that states workload, staffing, competing priorities, or the pendency of other reviews does not satisfy this paragraph**; and\n>\n> **(2)** where the environmental document is not complete by the deadline as extended, the court, on petition of the applicant, **shall** order the agency to complete the document as soon as practicable on a schedule not exceeding 60 days after the order issues, except that the court may set a longer period only to the extent a statute, or a rule published before the application was filed, requires the additional time; **workload, staffing, and competing priorities are not grounds for a longer period, and the agency shall complete the document as soon as practicable within the period set**; the court **shall** award the applicant its costs and fees on that petition, and the burden is on the agency to show cause why the document could not be completed.\n\n**(c) Operative in any event.** With respect to a covered clean energy project, paragraphs (1) and (2) of subsection (b) operate according to their terms whether or not the amendment made by subsection (b) takes effect.\n\n**(d) Publication by operation of law.** An applicant for a covered Federal authorization may tender its own final environmental document of a class required by law for the proposed action to the lead agency by any means the agency's published rules permit for submissions on the application or by certified mail to the head of the agency, and make the document publicly available without charge on a website identified in the submission. If, by the end of the period set in the schedule to this Act after the later of the lapse of the review deadline for that document under \u00a7 3(i) and the date on which the applicant has both tendered the document and made it publicly available, the lead agency has published neither that document nor its own final environmental document of the same class for the same proposed action, **the applicant's document is published by operation of this subsection on the day after that period ends, without any further act of any officer.** The applicant shall keep the document publicly available without charge. **The lapse is that of the review deadline as extended under subsection (b)(1), where an extension applies, and not the end of a court-ordered completion schedule; a schedule described in \u00a7 3(i) postpones neither the period of this subsection nor publication under it.** Notwithstanding section 107(f) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336a(f)), no supervision, evaluation, approval, adoption, certification, or other act of the agency is a condition of publication under this subsection. **A document so published is treated as the agency's final environmental document for every purpose, including judicial review, and the agency shall take responsibility for its contents and defend it in any action seeking review.** Publication under this subsection satisfies every condition in this Act that the document be published, including during a lapse in appropriations of the United States, but does not determine the document's sufficiency under law or foreclose its judicial review under \u00a7 7."},{"anchor":"sec-6","heading":"\u00a7 6. The decision clock","part":null,"body":"**(a) The two clocks are distinct.** The review clock of \u00a7 5 governs the completion of the environmental document. The decision clock of this section begins when the record on the application is complete and every environmental document the law requires has been published, including by operation of \u00a7 5(d). **Publication under \u00a7 5(d) satisfies the publication condition of this section without determining the document's sufficiency under law or foreclosing its judicial review under \u00a7 7.**\n\n**(b) Deemed grant.** Where the record on an application for a covered Federal authorization is complete, every environmental document required by law has been published, including by operation of \u00a7 5(d), and the agency has not granted or denied the application within the period set in the schedule to this Act, **the authorization is granted by operation of this subsection on the day after that period ends, on the terms stated in the application and subject to every condition of general applicability that a statute or a rule published before the application was filed attaches to that class of authorization, and no further act of any officer is required.** The agency shall issue the instrument evidencing the authorization as a ministerial act, and mandamus lies to compel the issuance.\n\n**(c) Denial must decide.** A denial within the period of subsection (b) must state each requirement of law the application fails and what would satisfy it. **A denial that states none is not a denial for purposes of subsection (b).**\n\n**(d) Refiled applications resume.** Where an application is withdrawn and refiled in substantially the same form, each review deadline and the period of subsection (b) run on the refiled application from the dates on which they began to run on the earlier application, excluding the days from the withdrawal to the refiling; the conditions of subsection (b) that the record be complete and every environmental document be published, including by operation of \u00a7 5(d), are determined on the refiled application, and where the period of subsection (b) has ended before those conditions are met, the authorization is granted on the day after the day on which they are met; a review deadline so determined is the applicable deadline under section 107(g) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969, notwithstanding paragraph (1) of that subsection; an extension made on the earlier application is the one extension \u00a7 5(b)(1) permits; a review deadline for a class of environmental document that did not run on the earlier application runs from the earliest date on which any review deadline began to run on it; and \u00a7 19(b) applies to the refiled application as it applied, or would have applied had it been pending on the date of enactment, to the earlier application. **An application is in substantially the same form where it concerns the same site and the same class of authorization, and no other test applies.** Where an application is denied and refiled in substantially the same form, and the refiled application cures each requirement the denial stated, the preceding rules apply to it with the date of denial substituted for the date of withdrawal, except that **the authorization is granted by operation of subsection (b) on the later of the date on which the preceding rules would grant it and the day after the minimum cure-review period set in the schedule to this Act ends, unless the agency has granted or denied the refiled application before that later date.** That minimum period begins when the application has been refiled, its record is complete, and every environmental document required by law has been published, including by operation of \u00a7 5(d); it does not restart any review period or the period of subsection (b). **A withdrawal and refiling in substantially the same form does not dispense with the minimum cure-review period**, and time that ran toward that period carries forward, excluding the days from withdrawal to refiling."},{"anchor":"sec-7","heading":"\u00a7 7. Judicial review of covered Federal authorizations","part":null,"body":"**(a) Time limit.** A claim seeking judicial review of a covered Federal authorization, or of an environmental document supporting one, is barred unless filed within 150 days after notice of the authorization or document is published. **Publication commences the period as to all persons, and the period is not subject to tolling, equitable or otherwise; the omission of any tolling rule is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(b) Expedition.** An action described in subsection (a) shall be set for expedited consideration, and the court shall issue its decision as soon as practicable.\n\n**(c) Remand without vacatur for curable defects.** Where the court finds a defect in an environmental document or in procedure that the agency can cure on remand, the court **shall** remand without vacating the authorization, and construction may proceed at the holder's risk, **unless the court finds that the specific activity to proceed during remand would cause harm that the cured document could not lawfully permit.** A defect of analysis, documentation, or explanation is curable; a want of statutory authority is not.\n\n**(d) The merits shall be reached.** No claim within subsection (a) shall be dismissed on a ground that prevents any court from ever reaching its merits, but nothing in this subsection directs the result on the merits of any claim."},{"anchor":"sec-8","heading":"\u00a7 8. Federal land","part":null,"body":"**(a) Application of the clocks.** Sections 5, 6 and 7 apply to an application for a right-of-way, lease, or other authorization for a covered clean energy project on land administered by the Secretary of the Interior or the Secretary of Agriculture.\n\n**(b) Priority areas are declaratory, not constitutive.** The Secretary concerned shall designate and maintain areas of low resource conflict for covered clean energy projects. **A designation is a convenience and not the source of any right: an application on land outside a designated area is processed under the same sections and the same clocks, and the absence, withdrawal, or revocation of any designation delays no application.**\n\n**(b-1) Rents are a duty, not a discretion.** Acreage rental rates and capacity fees for a covered clean energy project shall be set at fair market value by a method published before the application is filed. **Where no method has been published, the rate is the lowest rate charged for a comparable authorization during the preceding three years, and the absence of a published method delays no application.** Where a criterion stated in section 3003 of title 43, United States Code, is met, the reduction that section permits **shall be made**, and the determination whether a criterion is met is reviewable. **This subsection operates whether or not that section is amended, and the discretion that section states is not a ground for declining a reduction its criteria support.**\n\n**(c) Processing is ministerial past the clock.** No officer may condition the processing of an application described in subsection (a) on a review, concurrence, or approval not required by a statute or by a rule published before the application was filed. Time consumed by any such condition counts against each review deadline and the period of \u00a7 6(b), and extends neither."},{"anchor":"sec-9","heading":"\u00a7 9. The outer Continental Shelf","part":null,"body":"**(b) Amendment.** Section 8(p) of the Outer Continental Shelf Lands Act (43 U.S.C. 1337(p)) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(11) Security of an issued authorization.** **(A)** A lease, easement, or right-of-way under this subsection, and any approval issued under it, once issued, **may be suspended or canceled only by a court of the United States, on complaint of the Secretary, on proof of a ground stated in the instrument or of a substantial violation of this Act, and on no other ground.** A violation is **substantial** only if it concerns a term material to safety, environmental protection, or payment, and continues after written notice and a reasonable opportunity to cure.\n>\n> **\"(B)** The Secretary may order a suspension without a court order only upon a written finding of imminent and particularized danger to life, to property, or to the marine environment, stating the facts found. **A finding stated in conclusory terms, or resting on a ground applicable to the class of projects rather than to the particular project, does not satisfy this subparagraph.** Such a suspension **expires 30 days after it issues** unless a court of the United States, on complaint of the Secretary filed within that period, orders its continuation.\n>\n> **\"(C)** On cancellation other than for the holder's violation, the United States shall pay the holder the fair value of the canceled interest and the costs incurred in reliance on it. An amount paid under this subparagraph is treated, for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as an amount realized on a sale or exchange of the canceled interest.\n>\n> **\"(D)** This paragraph applies to an instrument issued before, on, or after the date of enactment of this paragraph.\"\n\n**(c) Operative in any event.** Subparagraphs (A) through (D) of the paragraph added by subsection (b) operate according to their terms, as limits on every officer of the United States, whether or not the amendment made by subsection (b) takes effect.\n\n**(d) Fisheries displacement.** From revenues received under section 8(p)(2) of the Outer Continental Shelf Lands Act with respect to a project, **the Secretary shall pay**, to each commercial fishing operation displaced from grounds it fished in the years preceding the lease, compensation computed at the rates set in the schedule to this Act. **Displacement is established by the logbook and vessel monitoring records already maintained under the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Management Act where such records exist for the operation, and otherwise by the documentation set in the schedule to this Act; no documentation beyond the applicable one may be required.** A claim denied in whole or in part may be brought in a district court of the United States, which determines the amount. **The rates are published before leasing, apply uniformly, and are not subject to negotiation project by project.** A payment under this subsection has the character, for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, of the fishing income it replaces.\n\n**(e) Shared transmission.** The Secretary shall offer, in each lease sale under section 8(p), the option of delivery through a shared transmission facility planned for the lease area, and shall publish, before the sale, the capacity, route, and cost-allocation method of any such facility."},{"anchor":"sec-10","heading":"\u00a7 10. Certified small energy systems \u2014 instant permitting","part":null,"body":"**(a) The national standard.** A certified small energy system that conforms to \u00a7 3(c) satisfies every requirement of a permitting authority as to the design, engineering, and equipment of the system. **No further design review is lawful, and subsection (c) states the consequence.**\n\n**(b) The Federal platform.** The Secretary of Energy shall operate, without charge to any jurisdiction or applicant, an automated system that verifies the conformity of an application to \u00a7 3(c) and transmits it to the permitting authority. **The platform is a convenience and not the source of any right: the absence, failure, or discontinuance of the platform delays no application and defeats no deemed grant**, and an application filed directly with a permitting authority proceeds identically.\n\n**(c) The rule for local process.** With respect to a certified small energy system \u2014\n\n**(1)** **no requirement of a permitting authority is effective** to the extent it requires design review, engineering review, or equipment approval beyond verification of conformity to \u00a7 3(c);\n\n**(2)** where a complete application is filed, the permit **is granted by operation of this paragraph** on the day after the period set in the schedule to this Act unless the permitting authority has before then denied it by a writing identifying the specific provision of \u00a7 3(c) the application fails; **a denial identifying none is not a denial**;\n\n**(3)** the fees of every kind charged for the application, the permit, and the inspection together may not exceed the amount set in the schedule to this Act; and\n\n**(4)** one inspection after installation may be required; a failed inspection must identify the specific nonconformity; and reinspection is limited to it.\n\n**(d) What this section does not reach.** This section does not displace \u2014 **(1)** a structural determination for a roof that a licensed professional certifies cannot bear the system; **(2)** a requirement of licensure for the person performing the installation; or **(3)** the application of a fire-access setback stated in the code adopted in the jurisdiction. **The exceptions to this section are those and only those stated in this subsection, and the omission of any other exception \u2014 aesthetic review, historic-district review beyond a district listed on the National Register, orientation, glare, or neighborhood character \u2014 is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(e) Election of State law.** A permitting authority whose own law grants a permit at least as quickly, at no greater cost, and with no additional design review, satisfies this section, and nothing in this Act requires any permitting authority to use the platform of subsection (b)."},{"anchor":"sec-11","heading":"\u00a7 11. Plug-in energy systems","part":null,"body":"**(a) Lawful nationally.** A plug-in energy system may be acquired, installed, and operated in every State. **No requirement of a permitting authority or of a utility is effective to require, with respect to a plug-in energy system, a permit, an application, an interconnection agreement, an approval, a fee, a meter change, or a notice** other than the one-time registration, if any, that the standard to which the device is listed itself requires.\n\n**(b) The utility may not penalize.** A utility shall not, by reason of a plug-in energy system, disconnect, refuse, surcharge, re-rate, or condition service to a customer, and a term of service to the contrary **is void.**\n\n**(c) The limit travels with the standard.** The power limit applicable to this section is the listed limit of the governing standard, as revised, under \u00a7 3(d). **No agency and no utility may set a lower limit.**"},{"anchor":"sec-12","heading":"\u00a7 12. Solar access","part":null,"body":"**(a) Private prohibitions void.** A covenant, restriction, condition of a homeowners' association, or term of a lease of a single-family dwelling, that prohibits or has the effect of preventing the installation or operation of a certified small energy system or a plug-in energy system on a dwelling or its lot, **is void, whenever the covenant, restriction, condition, or term was created.** A provision **has the effect of preventing** an installation where compliance would reduce the system's production, or raise its cost, by more than the tolerance set in the schedule to this Act. **No other test applies.**\n\n**(b) What survives.** A provision requiring that equipment not extend above a ridgeline, or that conduit be routed out of street view, survives subsection (a) where it is within the tolerance. **The provisions that survive are those and only those within this subsection.**\n\n**(c) Renters.** A term of a residential lease prohibiting a plug-in energy system on a balcony, patio, or window of the leased premises **is void**, except a term implementing a structural or fire-code determination described in \u00a7 10(d).\n\n**(d) Tribal law preserved.** This section does not displace the law of an Indian Tribe, and that omission is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-13","heading":"\u00a7 13. Distributed interconnection","part":null,"body":"**(a) Deemed approval.** Where a customer applies to a utility to interconnect a certified small energy system, and the utility does not, within the period set in the schedule to this Act, approve the application or deny it by a writing identifying the specific engineering constraint of the specific circuit, the interconnection **is approved by operation of this subsection**, and the customer may operate the system. **A denial identifying no circuit-specific constraint is not a denial, and a denial inconsistent with the utility's most recent map published under subsection (b) is not a denial.**\n\n**(b) Hosting capacity is published.** Each utility shall publish, and revise at least annually, a map of the hosting capacity of its distribution circuits. Publication is a ministerial duty, and mandamus lies to compel it.\n\n**(c) Informational right.** A customer denied interconnection is entitled to the study and data on which the denial rests, within the period set in the schedule to this Act, without charge.\n\n**(d) No charge by reason of the system.** A utility shall not, by reason of a customer's certified small energy system, impose on the customer any charge or term of service, or require the customer to take any rate schedule. **A charge, term, or requirement is imposed by reason of the system where a customer of the same rate class whose metered use is the same, and who has no such system, would not bear it; no other test applies.** A charge, term, or requirement contrary to this subsection **is void, whether or not it is stated in a tariff, rate schedule, or order approved or prescribed by a State regulatory authority or by the governing body of the utility**, and neither that approval nor any rule that a filed or approved rate binds the customer is a defense; an amount collected under it is an overcharge, which the utility **shall** refund to the customer with interest. **A refund under this subsection, other than its interest, is a reduction of the amount its recipient paid for electric service or for the interconnection of the system, and has that character for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.** For purposes of this subsection \u2014\n\n**(1)** **metered use** is the energy and demand the utility delivers to the customer, as the customer's meter records them in each interval in which it records them;\n\n**(2)** the customer's rate class is the class to which the utility would assign the customer if the customer had no such system, and **a class whose membership turns, in whole or in part, on the presence of such a system, or on a characteristic of metered use that such a system produces in the customer's case, is not a rate class**;\n\n**(3)** a requirement that the customer's meter record a quantity that the meters of customers of the class without such a system do not record, other than energy delivered to the utility, **is a requirement imposed by reason of the system, and a charge computed on that quantity is a charge so imposed**; and\n\n**(4)** a charge includes a fixed, demand, standby, capacity, or grid-access charge, a minimum bill, and a deduction from or offset against any amount owed to the customer, **regardless of form, title, or characterization**, and a charge or term imposed by reason of the system on a person who owns, leases, finances, or installed the system at the customer's premises **is imposed on the customer**, and that person may recover it as the customer may.\n\n**(e) What subsection (d) does not reach.** Subsection (d) does not govern \u2014\n\n**(1)** the price a utility pays or credits for energy the customer delivers to the utility, except that **the amount by which that price falls below the utility's avoided costs of energy delivered as available, as most recently determined for its purchases from qualifying facilities under section 210 of the Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act of 1978 (16 U.S.C. 824a-3), is a charge**, and, where no such determination exists, a price below zero is a charge;\n\n**(2)** a one-time charge, at actual cost, for a meter capable of recording energy in both directions, where the customer's existing meter cannot; or\n\n**(3)** a one-time charge, at actual cost, for an upgrade to the distribution system that the utility has identified in writing, within the period of subsection (a), by the specific engineering constraint of the specific circuit, as required for the interconnection, assessed as it would be assessed on any other customer whose interconnection required the same upgrade.\n\n**The exceptions to subsection (d) are those and only those stated in this subsection.**\n\n**(f) Relationship to section 210.** Subsections (d) and (e) are in addition to section 210 of the Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act of 1978 (16 U.S.C. 824a-3) and the rules under it, and a customer has the protection of whichever is greater. **That a rate applies to customers with similar load or other cost-related characteristics is not a defense under subsection (d)**, and the omission from subsection (d) of any such standard is a determination of Congress and not a gap. **No doctrine of primary jurisdiction is a ground to stay, refer, or dismiss a claim under subsection (d)**, which a court decides on the tariff and the customer's metered use."},{"anchor":"sec-13a","heading":"\u00a7 13A. State prohibitions of interconnected projects","part":null,"body":"**(a) The rule.** A law, ordinance, or requirement of a State or political subdivision **is not effective to prohibit** the construction or operation, on nonfederal land, of a covered clean energy project for which a completed interconnection request is pending with, or an interconnection agreement has been executed with, a transmission provider whose facilities serve interstate commerce, and for which site control has been demonstrated to that transmission provider.\n\n**(a-1) Request and site control.** For purposes of this section \u2014\n\n**(1)** an interconnection request is **completed** on the earlier of the date on which the transmission provider deems it valid or complete under the tariff, procedures, or rules governing it and the date on which the period those procedures allow the transmission provider to notify a deficiency ends with none notified, and it is **pending** from that date until it is withdrawn or deemed withdrawn under those procedures;\n\n**(2)** **site control** is the exclusive right of the person holding the interconnection request or agreement, directly or through an affiliate or trustee, by ownership, leasehold, option to purchase or lease, or other right to develop, to occupy land for the construction and operation of the project, and site control is **demonstrated** where documentation of it, to the extent the procedures governing the request or agreement require at its stage, or, where those procedures require none, documentation establishing it for the site of the project, has been submitted to the transmission provider, and the transmission provider has not determined in writing under those procedures that the person no longer holds it; **a deposit, affidavit, or other submission in lieu of site control is not a demonstration of it**;\n\n**(3)** subsection (a) reaches the project as described in the interconnection request or agreement, at the site described in it; and\n\n**(4)** the statement under penalty of perjury of the person holding the request or agreement, identifying the transmission provider, the request or agreement, the date on which the request was completed or the agreement executed, and the documentation of site control submitted and its date, served on the State or political subdivision by certified mail to its clerk or by any means its published rules permit for an application, is evidence of the facts it states.\n\n**(b) Prohibition is defined, and the definition is the whole reach.** A requirement **prohibits** a project only where it \u2014 **(1)** bans the project's technology by its terms; **(2)** imposes a moratorium exceeding the period set in the schedule to this Act; or **(3)** imposes siting requirements that, in combination, leave less than the fraction set in the schedule to this Act of the otherwise-suitable land in the jurisdiction available to the technology. **No other test applies, and a requirement outside this subsection is not within this section however burdensome, except as subsection (c-1) provides for the decision on a particular project.**\n\n**(c) The safe harbor, stated numerically.** A setback not exceeding the multiples of structure height set in the schedule to this Act, a noise limit not below the level there set, and any health, safety, building, fire, or electrical code of general application, **are not prohibitions under this section and are not displaced**, whatever their effect on a particular project.\n\n**(c-1) A project that meets the standards may proceed.** With respect to a project described in subsection (a) that conforms to the safe harbors of subsection (c) and to every objective standard of the State and of the political subdivision in which it is located \u2014\n\n**(1)** where the record on an application to a political subdivision of a State, or to an agency or instrumentality of one, for a permit, approval, or other authorization required for the construction or operation of the project is complete, the political subdivision, agency, or instrumentality being for this purpose a permitting authority, and its ordinance or published submittal requirement a rule, within the meaning of \u00a7 3(g), **the project may be constructed and operated as if the authorization had been granted** on the day after the period set in the schedule to this Act, on the terms stated in the application and subject to every objective standard, unless before then the authorization has been denied by a writing identifying each specific objective standard the project fails and the respect in which it fails it; **a denial identifying none is not a denial**, and no further act of any officer is required; for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, a project to which this paragraph applies holds the authorization from the day after that period; for purposes of this paragraph, an application, study, document, or fee is received on the date it is tendered to the political subdivision, agency, or instrumentality by any means its published rules permit or by certified mail to its clerk; a record on which it identifies no missing item in writing within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the last item is tendered is complete on the last day of that period, and such a writing identifies every item then missing, and an item it omits, an item already received, and an item required by no published rule do not delay completeness; no hearing, conference, certificate, authorization, or other act of the political subdivision, agency, or instrumentality or its officers is a study or document that is a condition of decision; applications for every authorization the project requires may be filed together; and a request for an inspection on which an authorization depends is an application, whose record is complete when the request is made and the work is ready for inspection;\n\n**(1A)** the applicant's statement under penalty of perjury of the dates of tender and of completeness, the end of the period of paragraph (1), and the absence of a denial, served on the political subdivision, agency, or instrumentality by any means its published rules permit for an application or by certified mail to its clerk, is evidence of the authorization for every purpose, and any requirement of evidence of the authorization is satisfied by it; no utility or transmission provider may refuse or delay interconnection, energization, or service for want of the authorization; and unless the political subdivision, agency, or instrumentality commences an action for a declaration of the stated dates, which lies in any court of competent jurisdiction, within 60 days after service, those dates are conclusive in every action and proceeding, and the statement states that rule on its face;\n\n**(2)** no requirement is effective to deny such an authorization, or to condition it, on any ground other than an objective standard the project fails, and a condition attached to such an authorization that no objective standard requires is not effective;\n\n**(3)** where an application is amended after a denial, a denial of the amended application may identify only a standard identified in the earlier denial or a standard that the amendment causes the project to fail, and the period of paragraph (1) runs on the amended application from the date its record is complete, except that, for an amendment that only cures a standard the denial identified, the record is complete when the amendment is tendered;\n\n**(4)** for purposes of this subsection, whether the project fails a standard identified in a denial is determined by the court de novo in any action or proceeding in which it is drawn in question, including an action under \u00a7 16, and a denial is not a denial for purposes of paragraph (1) where the project fails none of the standards it identifies; and\n\n**(5)** where the record on an application was complete before the date of enactment, the period of paragraph (1) begins on that date.\n\nAn **objective standard** is a requirement of law, published and in effect on the date the application was filed, that involves no personal or subjective judgment by a public official and is uniformly verifiable by reference to an external and uniform benchmark or criterion available and knowable by both the applicant and the official before that date. **A requirement that turns on compatibility with the character of an area, visual or aesthetic effect, public sentiment, property values, or the general welfare is not an objective standard, however it is stated, and no other test applies.** This subsection does not make effective a requirement that prohibits a project within the meaning of subsection (b), and does not confer on any person a right to construct or operate a project in violation of an objective standard.\n\n**(d) Express supersession.** Congress here states expressly its intent that subsection (a) supersede the law of the States and their political subdivisions to the extent and only to the extent subsection (b) states, and that subsection (c-1) supersede that law to the extent and only to the extent it states, and **no presumption against preemption applies to the construction of this section.** Congress states expressly, for purposes of every rule of construction requiring a clear or plain statement before a statute is read to reach the land-use authority of a State or political subdivision, or the allocation of authority between them, however denominated, that this section reaches that authority to the extent it states. The law of an Indian Tribe is not displaced, and that omission is a determination of Congress.\n\n**(e) Severability within this section.** The application of this section to each class of requirement in subsection (b), and to each technology, is severable from every other application; subsection (c-1) is severable from subsections (a) and (b), and each paragraph of subsection (c-1) from every other; the application of this section to a project for which no interconnection agreement has been executed is severable from its application to a project for which one has; and the safe harbor of subsection (c) survives any invalidation of subsection (a) or (c-1)."},{"anchor":"sec-14","heading":"\u00a7 14. Scope; substitution","part":null,"body":"**(a) One scope for the whole Act.** Sections 4 through 9 and section 13A apply only to covered clean energy projects. Sections 10 through 13 apply only to certified small energy systems and plug-in energy systems.\n\n**(b) Nothing else is accelerated.** Nothing in this Act modifies the review, permitting, or judicial-review provisions applicable to any facility that is not a covered clean energy project. Section 7 of the Natural Gas Act is not amended, enlarged, or diminished by this Act.\n\n**(c) Anti-substitution.** The limits of \u00a7\u00a7 10(c), 11(a), 11(b), 12(a), 13(a), and 13(d) apply to any requirement, term, or practice invoked to reach substantially the same result, **regardless of form, title, or characterization**, including a moratorium, a fee structure, an insurance requirement, an aesthetic guideline, or a study requirement."},{"anchor":"sec-15","heading":"\u00a7 15. No off switch","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Every deemed grant, voidness rule, and ineffectiveness rule in this Act operates without any rulemaking, guidance, form, designation, or other action by any agency or officer, and the absence of any such action suspends no provision of this Act.\n\n**(b)** **No agency is authorized to exempt any person, jurisdiction, or class from this Act**, to add to the exceptions of \u00a7 10(d) or \u00a7 12(b), or to lower the limit of \u00a7 11(c).\n\n**(b-1) Throughput, published.** Not later than December 31 of each year, each agency and permitting authority subject to \u00a7\u00a7 5 and 6 shall publish on its public website, for the fiscal year ending the preceding September 30, the following, for each office, for each State in which a project is located, the outer Continental Shelf being treated as a State, for each class of authorization, and for the agency as a whole \u2014\n\n**(1)** the number of applications received; the number of decisions made within the \u00a7 6 period and after it, stated separately as grants and denials; **the number of authorizations granted by operation of \u00a7 6(b) or \u00a7 10(c)(2)**; the number of applications withdrawn and refiled in substantially the same form under \u00a7 6(d); the number of applications denied for which an authorization of the same class for a site that includes any part of the site of the denied application was granted, by decision or by operation of law, during the year and within three years after the denial, with the time from the first complete record to that grant at the median and at the ninetieth percentile; and the number of environmental documents completed within the applicable period and after it, and the number pending past the applicable period at the end of the year, with the time since the filing of the application each has been pending at the median and at the ninetieth percentile, each stated separately for each class of period;\n\n**(2)** the time from the filing of an application to a complete record, to the completion of each environmental document, and to decision, and the time from a complete record to decision, **an authorization granted by operation of \u00a7 6(b) being decided on the day it is granted**, each at the median and at the ninetieth percentile; for applications with a complete record that are undecided at the end of the year, the time elapsed since the complete record at the median and at the ninetieth percentile; the number of written requests for further information; and the median time applicants took to answer them;\n\n**(3)** the numbers of paragraphs (1) and (2) for applications any part of whose periods ran during a lapse in appropriations of the United States, stated separately and not excluded, with the number of days of lapse; and, for each of the first three fiscal years ending after the date of enactment of this Act, the numbers of paragraphs (1) and (2) for applications pending on that date, stated separately from those for applications filed after it;\n\n**(4)** for each application decided after, or pending beyond, the applicable period or the \u00a7 6 period, the number of days on which the agency's records show that the application awaited a consultation, concurrence, or opinion that a statute requires of another Federal agency for the decision, naming that agency, which days remain in the numbers of the office publishing them; and\n\n**(5)** the number of full-time-equivalent reviewers and support personnel that the applications on which an environmental document or a decision fell due during the year, each counted once, together with the applications on which the applicable period or the \u00a7 6 period had been exceeded at the beginning of the year, require for completion within those periods, **computed at the workload benchmark set in the schedule to this Act for each class of work**, and the full-time equivalents of work charged during the year to the review and decision of those applications, as recorded in the agency's time-and-attendance records.\n\nFor purposes of this section: an **office** is the lowest organizational unit of the agency whose officer signs the decision or the environmental document; a **class of authorization** is the authorizations issued under one provision of law, and no agency may combine or divide classes for purposes of this section; the **applicable period** of an environmental document is its review deadline under \u00a7 3(i); and **the date of a complete record** is the date the agency recorded as that date and gave to the applicant, or, where it recorded and gave none, the date the application was filed. This subsection applies to every application within \u00a7\u00a7 5 and 6 whether or not the project is a covered project within the meaning of section 41001(6) of the FAST Act (42 U.S.C. 4370m(6)), and publication on the Permitting Dashboard maintained under section 41003(b) of that Act (42 U.S.C. 4370m\u20132(b)) does not satisfy it. A report under section 107(h) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336a(h)) does not satisfy this subsection, and neither that report nor its measure of a deadline limits the numbers this subsection requires.\n\n**(b-2) A shortfall is named.** Where the applications on which an environmental document or a decision fell due during the year and that exceeded the applicable period or the \u00a7 6 period, or were granted by operation of \u00a7 6(b), each counted once however many periods it exceeded, are more than the share set in the schedule to this Act of all applications on which an environmental document or a decision fell due during the year, each counted once \u2014 tested separately for each office, each State, each class of authorization, and the agency as a whole, and exceeded at any level at which it is exceeded \u2014 **that shortfall is evidence that the staffing for the review and decision of those applications at that level is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication**, together with the part of the share attributable to applications any part of whose periods ran during a lapse in appropriations of the United States. An application denied and later granted as subsection (b-1)(1) describes is counted, in the year of the grant, as an application on which a decision fell due and that exceeded a period. The test reads the numbers subsection (b-1) requires to be published, and an agency that has not published them by the date that subsection requires is treated as exceeding the share at every level. A statement under this subsection is a finding about the capacity of an office or agency and not about any application. In each year in which the share is exceeded at any level, the head of the agency shall transmit the statement and the figures of subsection (b-1)(5) to the committees of the Senate and the House of Representatives with jurisdiction over the agency and over its appropriations, at the same time they are published and notwithstanding section 1108(e) of title 31, United States Code, stating in the transmission why sums available under subsection (c) were not used to fill the positions those figures show to be required, and a statement that recites hiring limits, workload, or competing priorities, and does not identify for each position not filled the sums obligated for it and the act or omission that prevented the filling, does not satisfy this requirement; **no officer of any agency may impose a condition on or impair that transmittal, and the omission of any other limitation of that section is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(c) Resources and continuity.**\n\n**(1) The appropriation.** There are permanently appropriated to each agency such sums as are necessary for the receipt, review, and timely processing of applications subject to \u00a7\u00a7 5 and 6, to the Secretary of Energy such sums as are necessary for the operation of the platform under \u00a7 10(b), and to each agency such sums as are necessary for the duties of this subsection. The appropriation is available for the compensation of the officers and employees of the United States who perform those functions and for the employer contributions attributable to that compensation, including every Government or agency contribution under title 5, United States Code, and for the taxes attributable to that compensation, and each contract for the operation of the platform under \u00a7 10(b) is funded from it. A lapse in appropriations does not suspend any clock, deemed grant, or duty of publication under this Act. **That rule applies whether or not any officer or employee performs any function during the lapse, and does not depend on the operation of paragraphs (2) through (8).**\n\n**(2) Exempt and paid.** Each officer and employee of the United States who, during a lapse in appropriations of the United States, performs a function paragraph (1) funds is exempt from furlough as to that function and **shall be paid for that work, at the employee's standard rate of pay, from the appropriation of paragraph (1) on the ordinary schedule.** For any period for which the payment this paragraph requires is not made in full, the officer or employee shall be paid as section 1341(c)(2) of title 31, United States Code, provides for an excepted employee who is required to perform work, for work performed, and as it provides for a furloughed employee, for any time in which an officer of the United States prevented the performance, whether or not the officer or employee is an excepted employee within the meaning of section 1341(c)(1)(D) of that title or was furloughed; section 1341(c)(3) of that title applies to the officer or employee for that period as it applies to an excepted employee; and section 1341(c)(2) does not otherwise apply to such an officer or employee. Status under this subsection does not depend on any definition, guidance, or designation of the Office of Personnel Management or of the Office of Management and Budget.\n\n**(3) Continuation by operation of law.** Each officer and employee of the United States whose duties, at any time within the period set in the schedule to this Act before a lapse in appropriations of the United States begins, included a function paragraph (1) funds **continues to perform that function during the lapse by operation of this subsection and without designation by any officer**, and paragraph (2) applies to that officer or employee. The head of each agency employing such an officer or employee shall not furlough the officer or employee as to that function and shall provide for its performance, and a furlough notice or order to the contrary is without effect as to that function. For purposes of this section, where an agency is headed by a body of more than one member, the head of the agency is the chair of that body.\n\n**(4) Authority.** The performance during a lapse in appropriations of the United States of the functions paragraph (1) funds, and each obligation incurred in it, is authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of title 31, United States Code; an obligation or expenditure for that performance in the amount this subsection requires does not violate section 1517(a) of that title by reason of an apportionment, or of an amount permitted by regulations prescribed under section 1514(a) of that title, that makes available less than paragraph (5) requires; and none of sections 1341, 1342, and 1517 of that title is a defense to an action under subsection (d) to compel it.\n\n**(5) Apportionment.** The appropriation of paragraph (1) is not subject to apportionment delay, reserve, or programmatic withholding, and is available notwithstanding sections 1512 and 1513 of title 31, United States Code. No apportionment may make available in any period less than the amount required to pay the compensation of the officers and employees who perform the functions paragraph (1) funds, and during a lapse in appropriations of the United States those paragraphs (2) and (3) reach, together with the contributions and taxes paragraph (1) names that are attributable to that compensation.\n\n**(6) Identification.** The head of each agency employing an officer or employee whom paragraph (3) continues or would so continue identifies by position the officers and employees of the agency whom paragraph (3) continues, or would continue if a lapse in appropriations of the United States began on the date of the identification; publishes that identification on the agency's public website not later than ninety days after the date of enactment of this Act, within thirty days after any change, and on the first day of any lapse in appropriations of the United States; on each day of such a lapse, states on that website for each position the number of incumbents and the number performing duties under paragraph (3); and includes the identification in any plan the agency prepares for operations during a lapse in appropriations, whether or not such a plan is otherwise required or published. An identification that states categories or counts without positions does not satisfy this paragraph, and every person is entitled to the identification as published. **The identification records the operation of paragraphs (2) and (3) and is not a condition of either.**\n\n**(7) Acts during a lapse.** No action taken, and no authorization granted, under this Act during a lapse in appropriations of the United States is invalid, voidable, or subject to challenge by reason of the lapse, of the status under this subsection of any officer or employee who performed it, or of section 1341 or 1342 of title 31, United States Code. An application, and each study, document, and fee described in \u00a7 3(g), tendered to an agency during a lapse in appropriations of the United States is received on the date it is tendered. For purposes of \u00a7 6(a) and (b), an environmental document is published during a lapse in appropriations of the United States on the date the agency makes it available to the public on its website, and the want of a notice in the Federal Register during the lapse does not delay that date; the agency shall publish any such notice in the Federal Register not later than the fifth business day after the lapse ends. This paragraph does not change when the period of \u00a7 7(a) begins.\n\n**(8) Severability.** Each paragraph of this subsection, and each application of it, is severable from every other; if paragraph (3) is held invalid, paragraphs (2) and (4) through (7) remain in force as to every officer and employee paragraph (2) reaches, and paragraph (6) then identifies the officers and employees who perform the functions paragraph (1) funds.\n\n**(d) Enforcement of this section.**\n\n**(1)** The duties of subsections (b-1), (b-2), and (c) are ministerial and are owed to every person whom their nonperformance injures, including an applicant whose application was pending before the office concerned during the year for which a publication is owed, or during a lapse in appropriations in which a duty of subsection (c) was not performed; and the district courts of the United States have jurisdiction of an action in the nature of mandamus by any such person against the head of the agency concerned to compel their performance, **without regard to whether the duty is owed to the plaintiff within the meaning of section 1361 of title 28, United States Code.** The court shall issue the writ on a showing that a duty of this section was not performed and that its nonperformance injured the plaintiff, and neither the lapse in appropriations, the want of funds for any other function of the agency, the burden on the agency of performing the duty, the availability of a remedy under section 706(1) of title 5, United States Code, nor any discretion to withhold the writ is a ground to deny or defer it.\n\n**(2)** The court shall award a prevailing plaintiff reasonable attorney's fees and costs. A plaintiff prevails for purposes of this paragraph where the plaintiff obtains relief through a judicial order, including a temporary restraining order or preliminary injunction, or through a voluntary or unilateral change in position by the defendant after the action is filed, if the claim is not insubstantial. Neither exhaustion of any administrative remedy nor notice to any officer is a condition of an action under this subsection.\n\n**(3)** The omission of this section from \u00a7 16 is not a determination that it is unenforceable. An action under this subsection lies against an officer of the United States and is governed by this subsection and by title 28, United States Code, rather than by \u00a7 16, and the omission from this subsection of the State forum and removal bar of \u00a7 16(b) is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(4)** Neither chapter 71, 75, or 77 of title 5, United States Code, nor any other provision of law governing personnel actions channels, precludes, or displaces an action under this subsection by a person other than the officer or employee concerned, and the absence from those chapters of review for such a person is not a determination of Congress that review is unavailable.\n\n**(5)** The end of a lapse in appropriations does not render moot an action to compel a duty of subsection (c), including the identification of officers and employees, and a defendant asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the failure cannot reasonably be expected to recur in a later lapse. Congress finds that lapses in appropriations recur, and that a lapse is ordinarily too short for an action to compel performance during it to be fully litigated before it ends."},{"anchor":"sec-16","heading":"\u00a7 16. Enforcement","part":null,"body":"**(a) Civil action.** A person aggrieved by a violation of \u00a7 10(c), \u00a7 11, \u00a7 12, or \u00a7 13, by the application of a requirement made ineffective by \u00a7 13A, by a denial or condition that \u00a7 13A(c-1) makes ineffective or treats as no denial, by the enforcement against a project of a requirement of authorization that \u00a7 13A(c-1)(1) treats as granted, or by an attempt to enforce a term void under this Act, may bring a civil action for declaratory and injunctive relief, actual damages, the amount set in the schedule to this Act per violation, and reasonable attorney's fees and costs, **which the court shall award to a prevailing plaintiff.** The right and the action are conferred expressly by this subsection, and no presumption against the creation of a private right or remedy applies.\n\n**(b) Forum.** An action under this section may be brought in a district court of the United States or in any State court of competent jurisdiction; an action brought in a State court **may not be removed**; and a State court shall not decline the action on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States.\n\n**(c) No assignment.** Nothing in this Act authorizes any person to sue as an assignee, in whole or in part, of a claim of the United States.\n\n**(d) Arbitration and forum terms.** Section 9(f-1) and (f-2) of the Right to Quit Act apply to a claim under this Act as if the person asserting the claim were a worker, and if that Act is not in effect, those subsections apply as they read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(d-1) Pleading.** A complaint states a claim under this section where it alleges facts which, if true, would establish a requirement, term, or practice within \u00a7 10(c), \u00a7 11, \u00a7 12, or \u00a7 13 and its application or attempted application to the plaintiff, or a requirement that prohibits a project within the meaning of \u00a7 13A(b), or a denial or condition that \u00a7 13A(c-1) makes ineffective or treats as no denial, or the enforcement against a project of a requirement of authorization that \u00a7 13A(c-1)(1) treats as granted, and its application or attempted application to a project described in \u00a7 13A(a), by which application the plaintiff is aggrieved. A person who holds a legal or equitable interest in such a project or its site, or holds its pending interconnection request or is a party to its interconnection agreement, is aggrieved by the application of such a requirement, denial, condition, or enforcement to the project, and no test of proximate cause or zone of interests applies beyond that. **No heightened standard of pleading applies**, and knowledge and motive may be alleged generally.\n\n**(d-2) No preconditions.** Neither exhaustion of any administrative remedy, nor any local appeal, nor notice to any officer or agency, is a condition of an action under this section, and no claim under this section is channeled into any administrative scheme.\n\n**(e) Predicate facts.** The existence of a requirement, term, or practice made ineffective or void by this Act, applied against a person, **constitutes irreparable injury**, and the balance of equities and the public interest favor relief.\n\n**(f) Character of amounts.** An amount received under subsection (a) as damages replaces what it compensates and has that character for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986; statutory damages and fee awards are includible in gross income but are not wages and are not net earnings from self-employment.\n\n**(g) Defense of this Act.** In any action in which the validity or constitutionality of this Act, or of any application of it, is drawn in question and the United States has declined, or has failed after notice from the court, to defend it, **any State, and any person whom the provision in question protects or benefits, may intervene as of right to defend it**, and the court shall not enter judgment against the provision's validity without hearing an intervenor so appearing."},{"anchor":"sec-16a","heading":"\u00a7 16A. Conforming amendments","part":null,"body":"**(a) Removal.** Section 1445 of title 28, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(f)** A civil action arising under section 16 of the Clean Energy Speed Act may not be removed to any district court of the United States.\"\n\n**(b) The bar does not depend on the amendment.** Section 16(b) operates according to its terms as an Act of Congress expressly providing otherwise within the meaning of section 1441(a) of title 28, United States Code, whether or not subsection (a) takes effect.\n\n**(c) Fee awards.** Section 62(e) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(23)** The Clean Energy Speed Act.\"\n\n**(d) Purpose stated operatively.** An amount received by a person for attorney's fees and costs under section 16 **is not included in the person's gross income**, and this subsection applies whether or not the amendment made by subsection (c) takes effect."},{"anchor":"sec-17","heading":"\u00a7 17. Relationship to other law","part":null,"body":"**(a) Rights are a floor.** This Act does not preempt a law of a State, a political subdivision, or an Indian Tribe that permits an energy system more quickly, at lower cost, or with fewer conditions than this Act, and such a law governs to that extent.\n\n**(b) Process is a ceiling only as stated.** The only requirements governing the permitting, installation, interconnection, or operation of an energy system or project that this Act makes ineffective are those stated in \u00a7\u00a7 10, 11, 12, 13 and 13A, and the omission of any broader preemption of such requirements is a determination of Congress. **The supersession stated in those sections is express; Congress here states its intent to supersede the law of the States and their political subdivisions, and private instruments, to the extent and only to the extent those sections state as to the requirements they reach; and no presumption against preemption applies to their construction.** **Section 16, including its rules of forum, pleading, preconditions, predicate facts, and arbitration and forum terms as applied in a State court, and \u00a7 16A(a) and (b) supersede the law of the States and their political subdivisions, and any contract or other private instrument, to the extent they state; the limit of the preceding sentence does not reach them; subsection (c) does not limit them, and \u00a7 16(d) applies notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code; and no presumption against preemption applies to their construction.** Relief under \u00a7 16 against a State or an arm of a State includes, in every case, declaratory and prospective injunctive relief against the officer responsible for applying or enforcing the requirement, term, or practice, with reasonable attorney's fees as part of the costs; if an award of damages against it is held not authorized by this Act or beyond the power of Congress, that relief remains and every other application of \u00a7 16 is unaffected. **Subsection (c) does not limit \u00a7 7(a) or (c), including in an action under chapter 7 of title 5, United States Code.**\n\n**(c) No implied repeal.** This Act does not limit any right or remedy under any other law."},{"anchor":"sec-18","heading":"\u00a7 18. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder and every other application remain in force.\n\n**(b) The ladder is stated.** Sections 4 through 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, and 13A are severable from one another. Within \u00a7 10, the certification standard of subsection (a) and the platform of subsection (b) are severable from the rule of subsection (c), and remain in force if subsection (c) is held invalid as to any class of permitting authority. Within \u00a7 9, subsection (d) is severable from subsections (b) and (c). Within \u00a7 13, subsections (d) through (f) are severable from subsections (a) through (c), and the application of subsection (d) to each class of utility named in \u00a7 3(h) is severable from its application to every other."},{"anchor":"sec-19","heading":"\u00a7 19. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a)** This Act takes effect on the date of enactment, except that \u00a7\u00a7 10 through 13 take effect on the first day of the calendar quarter beginning after the period set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(b)** Sections 5 through 8 apply to an application pending on, or filed after, the date of enactment. For an application pending on the date of enactment \u2014\n\n**(1)** subject to paragraph (2), a review deadline running on that date keeps the date on which it began to run and the date on which it ends, and is not restarted, and, where no extension was made before that date, may be extended once under \u00a7 5(b)(1);\n\n**(2)** the extensions made before that date are together the one extension \u00a7 5(b)(1) permits, and the deadline runs to the earlier of the date the last of them set and the thirtieth day after the review deadline under \u00a7 3(i), without extension;\n\n**(3)** a period of \u00a7 5(d), \u00a7 6(b), or \u00a7 7(a) that would have begun before the date of enactment begins on that date, and a claim filed before the date of enactment is not barred by \u00a7 7(a); and\n\n**(4)** notwithstanding paragraphs (1) and (2) and \u00a7 5(a), where no event that would start the review period for an environmental assessment or an environmental impact statement under section 107(g)(1) of the National Environmental Policy Act of 1969 (42 U.S.C. 4336a(g)(1)) occurred before the date of enactment, or the earliest applicable event occurred before June 3, 2023, the review period for that document begins on the date of enactment, and **no time before enactment counts toward that period.**"},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 3(c)(1) certified small energy system capacity limit | [___ kW nameplate] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 3(c)(3) code-edition currency period | 3 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 5(b)(1) review-deadline extension maximum | 30 days, stated in \u00a7 5(b)(1) and \u00a7 19(b)(2) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 6(b) decision-clock period | [___ days] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 9(d) fisheries compensation rates | For each year of displacement, the operation's average annual ex-vessel revenue from the grounds from which it is displaced, over the five calendar years preceding the lease, stated in dollars of the year of payment by the implicit price deflator for gross domestic product published by the Bureau of Economic Analysis, multiplied by 100 percent for each year of construction and each year of decommissioning, and, for the years after construction ends, by 100 percent in the first, 80 percent in the second, 70 percent in the third, 60 percent in the fourth, and 50 percent in the fifth | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 10(c)(2) instant-permit period | 60 days after a complete application is filed | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 10(c)(3) instant-permit total fee cap | $450, plus $15 for each kilowatt of alternating-current nameplate capacity above 15 kilowatts, charged by every permitting authority together | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 12(a) solar access tolerance | 10 percent of production; for cost, the lesser of 10 percent of the cost of the system and $1,000, cost being measured before any credit, deduction, rebate, or other incentive | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 13(a) distributed interconnection period | 25 business days after the application is received, a business day being a day other than a Saturday, a Sunday, or a legal public holiday under section 6103(a) of title 5, United States Code | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 13(c) denial-data delivery period | 5 business days after the denial, a business day being as S-9 states | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 16(a) statutory damages per violation | [$___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 19(a) onset period for \u00a7\u00a7 10\u201313 | 90 days after the date of enactment | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 9(d) fisheries displacement documentation for operations without Federal records | The sworn statement of the person conducting the operation, made by that person or, where it is not an individual, by an officer, partner, or member authorized to bind it, which may be made by declaration under section 1746 of title 28, United States Code, identifying the grounds fished, each year in which the operation fished them, and the operation's ex-vessel revenue from them in each such year, together with such of the following as are available to that person: a harvester report or logbook filed with a State, by trip or by monthly summary; a landing receipt, fish ticket, or dealer report filed with a State; time-stamped vessel position records, including Automatic Identification System and electronic chart-plotter records; and an Internal Revenue Service Form 1099 or other tax document, a sales receipt, or a dealer settlement showing revenue. A record filed with a State includes the copy the State agency holds. Where a record states an area of catch that includes the grounds and extends beyond them, the revenue from the grounds is the share of the revenue the record shows for that area that the statement states, and not more than that revenue | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 13A(b)(2) moratorium period constituting prohibition | 180 days, counting the moratorium together with every extension or renewal of it | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 13A(b)(3) available-land fraction | [___ percent of otherwise-suitable land] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 13A(c) setback and noise safe harbors | [___ \u00d7 structure height; ___ dBA at the nearest existing dwelling] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 15(b-2) share of applications on which an environmental document or a decision fell due in a year that exceeded a period or were granted by operation of law, constituting a shortfall | 5 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 15(b-1)(5) workload benchmark for the staffing figure | [___ environmental documents or decisions per full-time-equivalent year, for each class of authorization and each class of environmental document] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 15(c)(3) pre-lapse duties period | [___ days before the lapse begins] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7 13A(c-1)(1) local decision period for a project meeting objective standards | [___ days after the record on the application is complete] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 13A(c-1)(1) local completeness-notice period | [___ days after the last item is tendered] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-22 | \u00a7 5(d) agency publication period before publication by operation of law | [___ days] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-23 | \u00a7 6(d) minimum cure-review period before a deemed grant on a refiled denied application | [___ days] | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-09"}}}};
